A group-based HPC can also operate to serve the needs of
others by quantifying how hypoxic conditions constrain
performance capabilities, and not just tolerance and resistance.
However, while a group-based HPC offers reasonably rapid
measurements on large numbers of ﬁsh, data on individual
variability are lost to a group average which may be suitable
for certain ecological questions. Comparisons become more
challenging too without any con ﬁdence limits on the data
(unless groups of ﬁsh are pooled to generate a mean and its
variance). Group-based HPCs operate with two assumptions.
